IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

§ `Singapore succession — the shared domain`

-- ===========================================================================
-- THE SHARED ONTOLOGY for Singapore's succession trio:
--
--   Wills Act 1838                      (WA1838)   -- is there a valid will?
--   Intestate Succession Act 1967       (ISA1967)  -- who takes, in what shares?
--   Probate and Administration Act 1934 (PAA1934)  -- who administers, and when?
--
-- WHY ONE ONTOLOGY AND NOT THREE ENCODINGS. The three Acts are not three
-- subjects. They are three views of ONE event -- a death -- and they share
-- their nouns: the same person is the PAA's "deceased", the Wills Act's
-- "testator" and the ISA's "intestate"; the same human being is a beneficiary
-- under a will, a next-of-kin under ISA rule 3, and a person "interested in
-- the estate" with standing to apply for a grant under PAA s 18(2). Encoding
-- each Act against its own private notion of a person would triplicate the
-- family tree and let the three copies disagree -- and the questions users
-- actually ask ("my father died without a will, who gets the flat, and how
-- long does it take?") cross all three Acts in a single breath.
--
-- So this module owns the nouns. Each Act module owns its own verbs, imports
-- these types, and never redeclares them.
--
-- INERT STYLE. Nothing here is exported. The types carry the statutes' own
-- words in their FIELD NAMES so a domain expert can read a record declaration
-- against the section it comes from. Citations ride on `@ref`.
--
-- WHAT THIS MODULE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO. It states no rule. A field here
-- records a FACT about the world ("the will was signed at the foot or end");
-- whether that fact makes the will valid is the Wills Act module's business.
-- The split is what lets the fork register point at one reading of one rule
-- without the ontology moving underneath it.
-- ===========================================================================


§§ `Persons, and the recursion the ISA needs`

-- ISA s 7 rule 3 distributes "by equal portions per stirpes ... amongst the
-- children ... and such persons as legally represent those children, in case
-- any of those children be then dead", and Proviso No. (2) pushes that to
-- "the remotest degree". A person is therefore a TREE, not a row: to divide
-- per stirpes you must be able to ask, of a predeceased child, who stands in
-- their place -- and of those, the same question again.
--
-- `issue` holds that person's own descendants. It is populated only where the
-- distribution needs it: a surviving child's issue never take (their parent
-- takes), so a surviving child may carry EMPTY issue without affecting any
-- answer.
DECLARE Person
    HAS `name`                    IS A STRING
        `survived the deceased`   IS A BOOLEAN
        `issue`                   IS A LIST OF Person

-- ISA s 6(a): a person "only conceived in the womb" at the date of death who
-- is "subsequently born alive" is treated as surviving. That is a fact about
-- the person, so it is recorded by setting `survived the deceased` TRUE --
-- the Act tells you how to classify, and classification happens at data-entry
-- time, not at rule time. Recorded here because a reader looking for s 6(a)
-- in the ISA module will not find a rule for it.


§§ `The deceased`

-- ISA s 2 excludes Muslims ENTIRELY from the Act; their estates are governed
-- by the Administration of Muslim Law Act 1966 and by Muslim law. This is not
-- a rounding error in an end-user tool -- it changes the answer completely --
-- so the flag is a required field rather than an optional one, and every
-- entry point is obliged to branch on it.
--
-- ISA s 4 and s 5 make DOMICILE and the MOVABLE/IMMOVABLE split jointly
-- decisive: movables follow the law of the domicile, Singapore immovables
-- follow this Act whatever the domicile.
DECLARE Deceased
    HAS `name`                        IS A STRING
        `date of death`               IS A DATE
        `domiciled in Singapore`      IS A BOOLEAN
        `Muslim`                      IS A BOOLEAN


§§ `The family`

-- The classes ISA s 7 rules 1-8 draw on, in the Act's own order. Each list
-- holds only persons in that class; whether they take is the ISA module's
-- question.
--
-- `surviving spouses` is a LIST and not a single person because ISA s 8
-- contemplates an intestate who "leaves surviving him more than one wife",
-- who then "share among them equally the share that the wife ... would have
-- been entitled to". A monogamous estate is the one-element case, not a
-- different shape.
DECLARE Family
    HAS `surviving spouses`   IS A LIST OF Person
        `children`            IS A LIST OF Person
        `parents`             IS A LIST OF Person
        `siblings`            IS A LIST OF Person
        `grandparents`        IS A LIST OF Person
        `uncles and aunts`    IS A LIST OF Person

-- NOT MODELLED, AND WHY. ISA s 3 defines "child" as "a legitimate child and
-- includes any child adopted by virtue of an order of court". An illegitimate
-- child is therefore NOT a "child" for ISA purposes and does not take on
-- intestacy. That is a live and consequential feature of the law, but it is a
-- CLASSIFICATION rule, not a distribution rule: it decides whether a human
-- being belongs in the `children` list at all. It is recorded in the fork
-- register rather than encoded as a predicate, because the tool cannot
-- observe legitimacy -- it can only be told.
--
-- ISA s 6(b) ranks half-blood relations "immediately after those of the whole
-- blood related to him in the same degree". Within rules 6 and 8 that matters
-- only where whole-blood relations in the same degree exist; see the ISA
-- module's note.


§§ `The estate`

-- ISA s 5 distributes "that property or the proceeds thereof, AFTER PAYMENT
-- THEREOUT of the expenses of due administration as prescribed by the
-- Probate and Administration Act 1934" -- so the ISA never divides the gross
-- estate. What it divides is what the PAA module computes.
--
-- The movable/immovable split is not presentational. Under ISA s 4(1)
-- movables are regulated by the law of the domicile; under s 4(2) immovables
-- are regulated by this Act "wherever he may have been domiciled". A
-- foreign-domiciled deceased with a Singapore flat is therefore the ordinary
-- case in which this Act governs PART of the estate and foreign law the rest.
DECLARE Estate
    HAS `immovable property in Singapore`  IS A NUMBER
        `movable property in Singapore`    IS A NUMBER
        `funeral, testamentary and administration expenses` IS A NUMBER
        `debts and liabilities`            IS A NUMBER


§§ `The will`

-- The facts the Wills Act tests. Every field is an OBSERVATION about the
-- document and its execution; the Wills Act module decides what they add up
-- to. Field names quote s 4, s 6(2), s 13 and s 27 so a reviewer can lay the
-- declaration beside the section.
DECLARE Will
    HAS `in writing`                                  IS A BOOLEAN
        `signed at the foot or end by the testator`   IS A BOOLEAN
        `number of attesting witnesses`               IS A NUMBER
        `witnesses present at the same time`          IS A BOOLEAN
        `witnesses subscribed in the testator's presence` IS A BOOLEAN
        `testator's age at execution`                 IS A NUMBER
        `soldier in actual military service or mariner at sea` IS A BOOLEAN
        `married after execution`                     IS A BOOLEAN
        `expressed to be made in contemplation of that marriage` IS A BOOLEAN
        `revoked by a later will, writing or destruction` IS A BOOLEAN
        `proportion of the estate beneficially disposed of` IS A NUMBER
        `an executor is appointed, and able and willing to act` IS A BOOLEAN

-- `proportion of the estate beneficially disposed of` is a SIMPLIFICATION,
-- and the only one in this ontology. ISA s 10 applies the intestacy rules to
-- "the part of his property not so disposed of", which in general requires
-- identifying WHICH property a will fails to dispose of -- a construction
-- question over the will's own text. This tool cannot read the will, so it
-- takes the undisposed part as a proportion of value: 1 means a will that
-- disposes of nothing, 0 a will that exhausts the estate. It is exact where
-- the gap is a residuary share and approximate where the gap is a specific
-- asset. Recorded in the fork register.


§§ `The case`

-- One record threaded as a single GIVEN parameter, per house style: an
-- ASSUME-shaped module typechecks but will not evaluate, and every question
-- this corpus answers is a question about one death.
DECLARE Case
    HAS `deceased`  IS A Deceased
        `family`    IS A Family
        `estate`    IS A Estate
        `will`      IS A MAYBE Will


§§ `Interpretation — the materialised forks`

-- R4 (fork representation): a fork that is MATERIALISED is one field of an
-- `Interpretation` record threaded as an ordinary GIVEN, so that both
-- readings are executable and the test suite can show what turns on the
-- difference. Forks resolved at encode time, or delegated to whoever supplies
-- the facts, are recorded in the fork register and do NOT appear here.
--
-- Each field is documented at the rule it bears on; see the ISA module.
-- `TYPICALLY` carries the orthodox reading as the field's default, which is the
-- slot a settling authority would be cited at if one ever settles the fork.
-- Both defaults are TRUE: both take the stirpital reading, which is the one the
-- fork register records as `live`.
DECLARE Interpretation
    HAS `stirpital division starts at the child generation` IS A BOOLEAN TYPICALLY TRUE
        `a sibling's children take per stirpes`             IS A BOOLEAN TYPICALLY TRUE

-- The reading this corpus takes unless a caller says otherwise. Fork-register
-- entries F1 and F3 carry the argument for each arm.
GIVETH AN Interpretation
`the orthodox reading` MEANS
    Interpretation WITH `stirpital division starts at the child generation` IS TRUE
                      , `a sibling's children take per stirpes`             IS TRUE

-- The competing reading of F1: where no child survives, division restarts per
-- capita at the nearest generation in which anyone did.
GIVETH AN Interpretation
`the per capita reading` MEANS
    Interpretation WITH `stirpital division starts at the child generation` IS FALSE
                      , `a sibling's children take per stirpes`             IS TRUE


§§ `Outcomes`

-- What every entry point in this corpus ultimately produces: who takes, how
-- much, and under which rule. `basis` carries the citation as inert text so
-- that a projection -- a ladder diagram, a DMN table, the wizard -- can show
-- the user WHY without re-deriving it.
--
-- `fraction` is a share OF THE DISTRIBUTABLE ESTATE, not a money amount.
-- L4 numbers are exact rationals, so a half really is 1/2 and the shares of
-- a complete distribution sum to exactly 1 -- a property the test suite
-- asserts rather than assumes.
DECLARE Share
    HAS `taker`     IS A STRING
        `fraction`  IS A NUMBER
        `basis`     IS A STRING

-- The shape of an answer. `governed by this Act` is FALSE where ISA s 2
-- (Muslim estates) or ISA s 4(1) (foreign-domiciled movables) puts the
-- question outside the Act; in that case `shares` is EMPTY and `basis`
-- names the regime that does govern, so the tool says "not my question"
-- rather than answering a question it was not asked.
DECLARE Distribution
    HAS `governed by this Act`  IS A BOOLEAN
        `shares`                IS A LIST OF Share
        `note`                  IS A STRING


§§ `Helpers over the ontology`

GIVEN c IS A Case
GIVETH A NUMBER
`gross estate of` c MEANS
    c's `estate`'s `immovable property in Singapore`
    PLUS c's `estate`'s `movable property in Singapore`

-- ISA s 5: what is distributed is what remains "after payment thereout of the
-- expenses of due administration as prescribed by the Probate and
-- Administration Act 1934". The PAA module owns the ORDER in which those
-- payments are applied (s 57 and the Second Schedule); the arithmetic of what
-- is left is common to every caller, so it lives here.
GIVEN c IS A Case
GIVETH A NUMBER
`distributable estate of` c MEANS
    `gross estate of` c
    MINUS c's `estate`'s `funeral, testamentary and administration expenses`
    MINUS c's `estate`'s `debts and liabilities`

-- PAA s 57(1) routes an INSOLVENT estate to the First Schedule (bankruptcy
-- priorities) and s 57(4) routes a solvent one to the Second Schedule. Which
-- applies is decided by this comparison and nothing else.
GIVEN c IS A Case
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the estate is solvent` c MEANS
    `gross estate of` c
    AT LEAST
      (c's `estate`'s `funeral, testamentary and administration expenses`
       PLUS c's `estate`'s `debts and liabilities`)

GIVEN people IS A LIST OF Person
GIVETH A LIST OF Person
`the survivors among` people MEANS
    filter (GIVEN p YIELD p's `survived the deceased`) people

-- NAMING NOTE. This reads `someone survived among` xs rather than the more
-- natural `any of` xs `survived`, because a mixfix pattern whose LAST token is
-- a keyword is accepted where it is DEFINED and then cannot be applied
-- anywhere: every call site reports "expects 1 argument, but you are applying
-- it to 2". Reduced to a three-line repro and reported upstream. Until that is
-- fixed, every mixfix in this corpus ends with an argument.
GIVEN people IS A LIST OF Person
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`someone survived among` people MEANS
    any (GIVEN p YIELD p's `survived the deceased`) people
